  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
    "lesson_core_move": "Match each term in an algebraically prepared $X(s)$ to a known one-sided Laplace transform pair, then use linearity to recombine the corresponding time-domain components into $x(t)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this step, your target is to map a prepared one-sided transform expression $X(s)$ into time-domain pieces. Each term in $X(s)$ must be recognized first, then converted to its matched known inverse pair before anything else is combined.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use linearity as the action rule: identify the template for each term, convert each term independently, then add the time-domain pieces back together. The smallest case is $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{1/(s+a)\\}=e^{-at}u(t)$ and $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{1/s\\}=u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)+G(s)\\}=f(t)+g(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then apply the scaled version term-by-term. If a term has an outside constant, keep that constant attached to the matched time-domain term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{kF(s)\\}=kf(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for the base check, $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{3/(s+2)\\}=3e^{-2t}u(t)$: first match $1/(s+2)$, then carry the scalar through.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Common failures are forgetting the $u(t)$ factor, changing the sign in the shift $s+a$, or matching after doing denominator algebra instead of before. Use this checklist: identify template, apply inverse pair, apply coefficients/signs, then sum outputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching a Single Prepared Term to a Known Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=7/(s+6)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Identify the term as a one-sided inverse-transform pattern first. Your cue is a rational term with denominator in the shifted form $s+a$, so you should match it against the template before doing any algebraic rearrangement.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the prompt term as $\\frac{7}{s+6} = 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s+6}$. This isolates the scalar from the canonical core $\\frac{1}{s+a}$ with $a=6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\\\left\\\\{\\\\frac{1}{s+a}\\\\right\\\\} = e^{-a t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the pair with linearity: for $k/(s+a)$, the inverse is $k e^{-a t}u(t)$. Here $k=7$ and $a=6$, so the time-domain term is $7e^{-6t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $x(t)=7e^{-6t}u(t)$. Keep the sign in the exponent and the one-sided factor $u(t)$ exactly as shown; this is the single new wrinkle from the tutorial because the coefficient is non-unit while the base term structure remains the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Inverting a Sum of Prepared Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=4/(s+2)+3/(s+5)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $X(s)$ is already prepared as a sum of standard inverse-transform templates, so the move is to split, invert, and then recombine by linearity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First split the transform into separate prepared terms: $4/(s+2)$ and $3/(s+5)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+2}+\\frac{3}{s+5}=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the template from the previous steps to each term: for each $a>0$, $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\}=e^{-a t}u(t)$. Thus, $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{4/(s+2)\\}=4e^{-2t}u(t)$ and $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{3/(s+5)\\}=3e^{-5t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Finally, combine the inverse components in the time domain by linearity to get one result: $x(t)=4e^{-2t}u(t)+3e^{-5t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not invert the sum as a whole before decomposition. Keep each term explicit in $s$-domain, then attach one $u(t)$ factor to each causal component before adding.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using Linearity with Scaled Inverse Transform Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=4/(5(s+1))+7/(2(s-2))-3/(s+4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this as a coefficient-check step: the cue is a sum in $X(s)$ where each term has a scalar multiplier and one is signed negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{4}{5(s+1)}+\\frac{7}{2(s-2)}-\\frac{3}{s+4}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same one-sided pair template to each prepared fraction: $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\}=e^{-a t}u(t)$. This gives matched pairs for $a=1$, $a=-2$, and $a=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Bring each coefficient outside its corresponding inverse and keep the sign on the third term: $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{4/(5(s+1))\\}=\\frac{4}{5}e^{-t}u(t)$, $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{7/(2(s-2))\\}=\\frac{7}{2}e^{2t}u(t)$, $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{-3/(s+4)\\}=-3e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{4}{5}e^{-t}u(t)+\\frac{7}{2}e^{2t}u(t)-3e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $x(t)=\\frac{4}{5}e^{-t}u(t)+\\frac{7}{2}e^{2t}u(t)-3e^{-4t}u(t)$. The only control move is coefficient and sign bookkeeping before summing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Inverse Laplace Transform by Known Pairs",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recombining a Prepared Partial-Fraction Form into $x(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=2/(s+1)-1/(s+4)+3/(s-2)$, compute $x(t)$ and combine the time-domain terms.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a recombination step: first finish each inverse map on the prepared partial-fraction terms, then add the three time-domain pieces into one $x(t)$ expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{3}{s-2}=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}-1\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same template $\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{1/(s+a)\\}=e^{-at}u(t)$ term by term. With $a=1$, $a=4$, and $a=-2$, the three inverses are $2e^{-t}u(t)$, $-e^{-4t}u(t)$, and $3e^{2t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+^{-1}\\{X(s)\\}=2e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-4t}u(t)+3e^{2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final coherent inverse is $x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-4t}u(t)+3e^{2t}u(t)$. All terms keep their own coefficient, sign, and $u(t)$ factor, and signs are checked before final sum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A common failure here is changing the sign in $e^{-a t}$ by treating every pole as decaying; keep $a=-2$ explicitly to get $e^{2t}u(t)$ for the $1/(s-2)$ term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
